    "knowledge_point": "Simplifying a Rational Expression Using the Pythagorean Identity",
    "problem": "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we factor $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot 1+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$",
